1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:04,223
Welcome to the Modern Java Enterprise Handbook.

2
00:00:04,223 --> 00:00:10,776
This is chapter thirty-nine: the foreign function and memory API, and the Vector API.

3
00:00:11,226 --> 00:00:13,660
Java had two long-standing gaps.

4
00:00:13,660 --> 00:00:20,048
Calling native code was so painful that people used sun dot misc dot Unsafe instead.

5
00:00:20,048 --> 00:00:24,915
And the SIMD instructions in every modern CPU were out of reach.

6
00:00:24,915 --> 00:00:27,044
The first gap is now closed.

7
00:00:27,044 --> 00:00:29,402
The second is still incubating.

8
00:00:30,302 --> 00:00:40,912
Calling a native library meant JNI: a C file, a generated header, a shared library built per platform, and a build pipeline for all of it.

9
00:00:40,912 --> 00:00:45,449
And every mistake was a JVM crash rather than an exception.

10
00:00:45,899 --> 00:00:57,690
Off-heap memory meant a direct ByteBuffer, capped at two gigabytes and freed only when the GC got round to it, or Unsafe, with no bounds checks and no lifetimes.

11
00:00:57,690 --> 00:01:00,107
A use-after-free crashed the JVM.

12
00:01:00,557 --> 00:01:02,332
And SIMD was invisible.

13
00:01:02,332 --> 00:01:11,053
A CPU can add eight integers in one instruction, but Java could only write a loop and hope the JIT vectorised it.

14
00:01:11,953 --> 00:01:17,723
The foreign function and memory API, final in Java 22, has five concepts.

15
00:01:17,723 --> 00:01:22,939
An Arena is a lifetime: when memory is freed and who can touch it.

16
00:01:23,389 --> 00:01:28,637
A MemorySegment is a region with a known size and lifetime, on or off the heap.

17
00:01:29,087 --> 00:01:33,759
A MemoryLayout describes its structure: fields, padding and arrays.

18
00:01:34,209 --> 00:01:38,497
A VarHandle gives typed access derived from the layout, with checks.

19
00:01:38,947 --> 00:01:42,937
And the Linker binds a native function to a Java method handle.

20
00:01:43,387 --> 00:01:46,579
The design point is that safety isn't optional.

21
00:01:46,579 --> 00:01:51,199
Every access checks bounds and checks that the arena is still alive.

22
00:01:51,199 --> 00:01:55,479
So a use-after-free becomes an exception, not a corrupted heap.

23
00:01:55,479 --> 00:01:58,128
That's the real difference from Unsafe.

24
00:01:59,028 --> 00:02:05,631
Allocate eight megabytes of off-heap longs in a confined arena, inside try-with-resources.

25
00:02:05,631 --> 00:02:11,721
They're freed at the closing brace, deterministically, not whenever the GC decides.

26
00:02:12,171 --> 00:02:20,257
And if a segment escapes its arena and you touch it afterwards, you get an IllegalStateException, already closed.

27
00:02:20,257 --> 00:02:24,907
With Unsafe, the same mistake corrupts memory or crashes the JVM.

28
00:02:25,357 --> 00:02:34,736
Confined arenas are the default choice: deterministic freeing, and single-thread confinement that makes the lifetime check cheap.

29
00:02:34,736 --> 00:02:40,333
Shared arenas allow any thread, and automatic arenas leave freeing to the GC.

30
00:02:41,233 --> 00:02:45,457
A C struct is described once as a layout, with named fields.

31
00:02:45,457 --> 00:02:52,214
VarHandles then give typed, checked access to each field, with offsets computed from the layout.

32
00:02:52,214 --> 00:02:56,508
So adding a field doesn't silently shift every read after it.

33
00:02:56,958 --> 00:03:02,692
Calling native code needs no C file, no generated header and no platform build.

34
00:03:02,692 --> 00:03:09,296
Describe the function's signature, look up the symbol, and call it through a method handle.

35
00:03:09,296 --> 00:03:11,401
Here, the C library's strlen.

36
00:03:11,851 --> 00:03:23,221
For a real library, jextract reads the C headers and generates all the layouts and handles, removing the most error-prone step: transcribing signatures by hand.

37
00:03:24,121 --> 00:03:27,021
Native access is a restricted operation.

38
00:03:27,021 --> 00:03:33,836
From Java 24, using it without permission prints a warning, and it's heading towards an error.

39
00:03:33,836 --> 00:03:36,374
Grant it with enable-native-access.

40
00:03:36,374 --> 00:03:43,044
It's the same integrity-by-default direction as the encapsulation changes in chapter eleven.

41
00:03:43,494 --> 00:03:54,587
Compared to the alternatives, memory segments have no two-gigabyte limit, free deterministically, describe struct layouts, and make native interop first class.

42
00:03:55,037 --> 00:03:58,075
But the honest default is still a plain array.

43
00:03:58,075 --> 00:04:07,517
Go off-heap only when the data is large, long-lived, and GC pressure is measured to be the problem, or when you must share it with native code.

44
00:04:08,417 --> 00:04:11,056
The Vector API makes SIMD explicit.

45
00:04:11,056 --> 00:04:20,328
Pick the CPU's preferred vector species, load a vector's worth of integers at a time, add, and reduce the lanes at the end.

46
00:04:20,328 --> 00:04:23,946
Then handle the leftover tail with a plain loop.

47
00:04:23,946 --> 00:04:27,489
On AVX2, that's eight integers per instruction.

48
00:04:27,939 --> 00:04:35,454
But it's still in an incubator module, jdk dot incubator dot vector, where it has been since Java 16.

49
00:04:35,454 --> 00:04:41,778
It's waiting on Project Valhalla's value types, so vectors don't cost an object each.

50
00:04:41,778 --> 00:04:48,697
Incubator APIs will change, so keep it behind your own interface and out of shared libraries.

51
00:04:49,597 --> 00:04:50,987
Production rules.

52
00:04:50,987 --> 00:04:53,767
Use FFM instead of JNI and Unsafe.

53
00:04:53,767 --> 00:04:57,201
Use confined arenas in try-with-resources.

54
00:04:57,201 --> 00:05:02,679
Describe memory with layouts and VarHandles, not offset arithmetic.

55
00:05:02,679 --> 00:05:05,295
Use jextract for real libraries.

56
00:05:05,295 --> 00:05:08,157
And grant native access explicitly.

57
00:05:08,607 --> 00:05:10,871
Never write new code on Unsafe.

58
00:05:10,871 --> 00:05:14,085
Never go off-heap without a measured reason.

59
00:05:14,085 --> 00:05:16,861
Never let segments escape their arena.

60
00:05:16,861 --> 00:05:20,660
And never put incubator APIs in published libraries.

61
00:05:21,560 --> 00:05:22,284
To recap.

62
00:05:22,284 --> 00:05:28,642
The foreign function and memory API, final in Java 22, replaces JNI and Unsafe.

63
00:05:28,642 --> 00:05:33,874
Arenas give deterministic lifetimes, and every access is checked.

64
00:05:33,874 --> 00:05:38,542
Layouts describe structs, and jextract generates bindings.

65
00:05:38,542 --> 00:05:43,853
And the Vector API makes SIMD explicit, but it's still incubating.

66
00:05:44,303 --> 00:05:48,934
Next is chapter forty, the last in this batch: the HTTP client.

67
00:05:48,934 --> 00:05:50,405
Thanks for watching.
